# Remote-CI Backlog Closeout

Use this class-level workflow when a user asks to complete backlog implementation, forbids local builds, requires a commit and push, and wants GitHub Actions as build proof.

## Core distinction

Separate three states:

- `in_progress`: implementation or verification is still underway.
- `done`: execution criteria are complete and the implementation has remote build proof.
- `closed`: every execution and closeout criterion has been checked with real evidence.

A green container build proves compilation, tests included by that workflow, packaging, and publication steps only to the extent the workflow actually runs them. It does not prove a post-fix live UI/session exercise, runtime log artifact, injected-failure scenario, or screenshot unless those were actually performed.

## Procedure

1. Load the project-scoped backlog items and preserve their project id, priority, provenance, and ordering.
2. Trace the implementation paths and make scoped changes. Do not run Docker, Podman, CMake, or another local build when the user forbids local builds.
3. Run only allowed non-build checks, such as `git diff --check`, and document their scope.
4. Stage only intended files; leave unrelated untracked files untouched. Commit and push the requested branch.
5. Select the GitHub Actions run by exact branch, event, and pushed `headSha`. Do not use an older successful run or a same-SHA PR run as proof for a push run when push proof was requested.
6. Poll the same run until top-level `status=completed` and `conclusion=success`. Require every required architecture, build, test, manifest, and publish job to succeed. Long C++ matrix builds can take hours; partial green jobs are not final proof.
7. Record the exact run URL, verified head SHA, terminal status/conclusion, and required job results in backlog notes.
8. Check only criteria backed by evidence actually collected. Source inspection and CI compilation cannot substitute for a post-fix runtime exercise.
9. Move to `done` when execution criteria and remote build proof are complete. Close only when all closeout criteria are genuinely evidenced. If `close` rejects incomplete criteria, leave the item open/done as appropriate and name the missing evidence instead of forcing closure.

## Evidence discipline

For every checked closeout criterion, retain a concrete proof source: exact command output, CI run URL/job, runtime log window, reproducible artifact path, API response, test result, or screenshot. If a criterion was not exercised, leave it unchecked and add a note naming the next required action.

## Pitfalls

- Do not mark runtime-only criteria complete because code inspection looks correct.
- Do not infer success from a run that is still `in_progress`, even if all visible jobs except one are green.
- Do not replace a slow exact-SHA run with an older run or a PR run.
- Do not claim a backlog item is closed when the CLI says criteria are incomplete.
- Do not stage `.claude/`, secrets, environment files, or unrelated untracked content.

See `references/remote-ci-evidence-checklist.md` for the compact command and reporting checklist.
